  data:  # ddf3
    kind: invariant  # ddf3
    title: "INV: FRAMEWORK_PATTERNS concept-tag schema and producer/consumer coupling"  # ddf3
    status: violated  # ddf3
    priority: 3  # ddf3
    parent:  # ddf3
    tags:  # ddf3
    - analysis_quality  # ddf3
    - cross_language_linkers  # ddf3
    - framework_dispatch_linkers  # ddf3
    isbefore: []  # ddf3
    duplicate_of: []  # ddf3
    not_duplicate_of: []  # ddf3
    pr_ref:  # ddf3
    description: "Filed as a follow-up finding from the ADR-0003-ext (Linker Subcategory Restoration) retrospective. The conversation that produced this item walked through the two-directory layout:\n\n  packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/frameworks/  (105 YAML files)\n  packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/linkers/     (45 Python files; 16-18 Framework-subcategory)\n\nThe Framework-subcategory linker <-> framework-YAML pairing was initially described as 'undeclared anywhere', which was incorrect. The pairing IS declared — inline in each linker's filter logic (matching a string literal against symbol.meta['concepts']). But the shape of that metadata is inconsistent across linkers (list[str] vs list[dict]), and no registry or documentation enumerates which concept names exist, which YAMLs emit each, or which linkers consume each.\n\nProposed resolution (two phases):\n\nPHASE 1 — SURVEY (no code change, produces a tracker sub-item or ADR amendment):\n\n  a. Audit frameworks/*.yaml for every 'concept:' emission and record (yaml_file, concept_name, emission_shape).\n  b. Audit linkers/*.py for every concept filter and record (linker_file, concept_name, expected_shape).\n  c. Cross-reference: identify concepts emitted but never consumed (inert tags), concepts consumed but never emitted (no-op linkers), and shape mismatches.\n  d. Write the findings to ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/concept_tag_audit_<date>.md.\n\nPHASE 2 — FORMALISATION:\n\n  a. Define a single shape for concepts in meta (proposal: list[dict] with mandatory 'concept': str field, plus optional concept-specific fields like path/method/queue). The dict form subsumes the string form and carries more information.\n  b. Migrate all Framework-subcategory linkers to the canonical shape.\n  c. Add a lightweight concept registry — docs/CONCEPTS.md or a section of LINKERS.md — enumerating every concept name, which YAMLs emit it, and which linkers consume it. This registry becomes the structural pairing that is currently invisible.\n  d. Add a schema-validation check (the framework_patterns.py enrichment layer already enforces schema on the YAMLs; extend it to the emitted meta.concepts shape).\n  e. Optional: add a declarative 'consumes_concepts: [...]' field to each Framework-subcategory linker module's header, and a matching 'emits_concepts: [...]' field to each YAML. These make the coupling self-documenting at both ends.\n\nAcceptance criteria for marking this invariant 'satisfied':\n\n  1. Every Framework-subcategory linker uses exactly one shape for its meta.concepts filter (pick one, document it).\n  2. docs/CONCEPTS.md (or equivalent) exists and is not a stub — it enumerates every concept name currently in use, maps each to its emitting YAMLs and consuming linkers, and flags any orphans as an open issue.\n  3. A test or pre-commit check verifies that for each linker filter (grep for concept-name string literals), at least one YAML in frameworks/ emits that concept — and conversely, every concept emitted by a YAML has at least one consumer.\n  4. A fresh dogfooding run (hypergumbo on itself, or on one of the bakeoff repos) shows edges emitted by each of the Framework-subcategory linkers — confirming the coupling is not silently broken on at least one test corpus.\n\nRelated to INV-miloj (umbrella URL-folding invariant), WI-gupah / WI-nutav / WI-lisov (specific Framework linker proposals — each implicitly adds new concepts to the vocabulary), and ADR-0003-ext (linker subcategory restoration; this invariant covers the producer-consumer contract that the subcategory vocabulary assumes).\n\nNot blocking on anything; actionable today. Phase 1 (the audit) is ~1-2 hours of grep and cross-reference, appropriate for a subagent pass using the ADR-0003-ext audit methodology (registered predictions + blind classification + null-finding search)."  # ddf3
    fields:  # ddf3
      statement: "The concept-tag metadata that flows from FRAMEWORK_PATTERNS (frameworks/*.yaml) to Framework-subcategory linkers (linkers/*.py) must have a single, formally-specified shape; every producer and every consumer uses that shape; and the set of concept names in active use is listed in one place that both sides can audit against. At any time, a reader of a YAML file should be able to see which linker will consume each concept name, and a reader of a linker file should be able to see which YAMLs emit each concept name the linker filters on."  # ddf3
      root_cause: "The concept-tag vocabulary was never formalised when the three-layer architecture from ADR-0003 (analyzer / FRAMEWORK_PATTERNS / linker) was implemented. The contract between frameworks/*.yaml (105 files) and the Framework-subcategory linkers in linkers/*.py (~16-18 files) is held together by matching string literals in two places:\n\n- YAML emits metadata with a 'concept' field (e.g. 'concept: route', 'concept: model', 'concept: middleware', 'concept: command', ...).\n- Each Framework-subcategory linker filters symbol.meta.get('concepts', []) for specific strings.\n\nTwo concrete problems surfaced by grep-level inspection on 2026-04-16:\n\nProblem 1 — DATA-REPRESENTATION INCONSISTENCY. Linkers handle 'concepts' in at least two incompatible shapes:\n\n  middleware_chain.py (line ~81): 'if \"middleware\" not in concepts' — treats concepts as list[str].\n  orm.py             (line ~86): 'if concept.get(\"concept\") == \"model\"' — treats concepts as list[dict].\n  subprocess_cli.py  (line ~305): 'c.get(\"concept\") == \"command\"' — treats concepts as list[dict].\n  http.py            (line ~1149): '_has_route_concept' calls 'c.get(\"concept\") == \"route\"' — treats concepts as list[dict].\n  openapi.py         (line ~256): same dict-based filter as http.py.\n\nThis implies the FRAMEWORK_PATTERNS enrichment layer is emitting concepts in both shapes (otherwise some of these linkers would silently match nothing). If a YAML emits 'concept: middleware' as a plain string and orm.py were to look at the same file, its dict-based filter would fail; conversely, middleware_chain.py would miss any middleware tagged via the dict form. We do not know today which YAMLs emit which shape, or whether the shapes are interchangeable at the framework_patterns.py enrichment-emit layer.\n\nProblem 2 — PRODUCER/CONSUMER COUPLING IS INVISIBLE. No linker module's docstring lists which concept strings it filters on or which YAMLs emit those concepts. No YAML file declares which linker(s) will consume its concept tags. No registry or markdown page enumerates the concept vocabulary. The coupling is real and enforced (as the grep evidence above shows) but only exists in the form of string literals at each consumption site, so:\n\n  - A YAML that emits 'concept: foo' where no linker filters for 'foo' is silently inert — the tag propagates but is unused.\n  - A linker that filters for 'concept: foo' where no YAML emits 'foo' is silently a no-op on every repo — no error, no warning.\n  - Renaming a concept (e.g. 'model' -> 'db_model') in a YAML without updating the linker produces silent coverage loss.\n  - Neither side detects the drift at review time, because the other end of the pairing is invisible from where the change is being made.\n\nEvidence of at least one manifestation: the two-shape inconsistency in Problem 1 would silently cause match failures today between YAMLs emitting the 'wrong' shape and linkers expecting the other. This is a violated invariant — not just a documentation gap."  # ddf3
